#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf13639-8d18-5909-a26d-aeda1e011ac0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Without Simplification"}] :question/id #uuid "75ac9866-6b0f-5736-a592-0bade16b8814" :question/math-academy-id "e-2333" :question/problem "Winston drove $200$ kilometers in two days. If he drove $110$ kilometers on the first day, what percentage of the total distance did he drive on the first day?" :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n- the part is the number of kilometers Winston drove the first day, which is $110$, and\n- the whole is the total number of kilometers he drove, which is $200$.\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = 110 \\div 200 = \\dfrac{110}{200}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{110}{200} = \\dfrac{110 \\div 2}{200 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{55}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{55}{100} = 55 \\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, Winston drove $55 \\%$ of the total distance on the first day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179497 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f67408f-f019-5365-b4a0-6f60ac23cc1d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Using Simplification"}] :question/id #uuid "2f3270cc-d191-5282-b9f7-cf63dcd69d70" :question/math-academy-id "e-2334" :question/problem "In his previous youth baseball season, Matt hit $75$ extra-bases, $9$ of which were home runs. What percentage of extra-base hits were home runs?" :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n- the part is the number of home runs, which is $9$, and\n- the whole is the total number of extra-base hits Matt connected, which is $75$.\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = 9 \\div 75 = \\dfrac{9}{75}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{75}=\\dfrac{9 \\div 3}{75 \\div 3}=\\dfrac{3}{25}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{25} = \\dfrac{3 \\times 4}{25 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{12}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{100} = 12 \\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12 \\%$ of extra-base hits were home runs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf13639-8d18-5909-a26d-aeda1e011ac0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Without Simplification"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3232bd3-359b-53ab-952e-6fe00f2e3427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178985} {:answer/id #uuid "c88f4b65-d203-5e9b-91b8-0b6e3f72c021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178986} {:answer/id #uuid "122f2591-2572-58fe-ad18-214dfc688121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178987} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc9d0c7-9fe5-5e91-a6bc-d8a400aa2938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178988} {:answer/id #uuid "7536fd18-fd67-5468-8f24-8ec13351d149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math